hend:

**arcedit** changes archives in place, without unpacking and repacking
them: rename, delete and add entries, and change their comments (file
notes) and protection bits. It is a Shell command, so file managers and
scripts can use it too, like they use LhA.

### What it can do

| Format | Delete | Rename | Comment | Protection | Add |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| LHA (.lha, .lzh) | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes, packed |
| ZIP (.zip) | yes | yes | yes | yes | yes, packed |
| LZX (.lzx) | yes, except part of a merged group | yes | yes | yes | yes, stored |
| TAR (.tar) | yes | yes | yes | owner rwe | yes |
| TGZ (.tgz, .tar.gz) | yes | yes | yes | owner rwe | yes, packed |
| GZIP (.gz) | - | stored name | yes | - | replaces the file |

- Only the changed parts are written: a delete copies the other entries
  byte for byte, a rename or comment rewrites only that entry's header, an
  add packs only the new files. Changing one comment in a 100 MB archive
  takes a moment, not a full repack.
- Self-extracting LHA archives and all LHA header levels (0, 1, 2) work.
- Drawers: deleting or renaming a drawer changes everything in it; added
  drawers come with all their contents.
- Added files keep their protection bits, file note and date.
- **Safe**: the new archive is written to a temporary file next to the old
  one and replaces it only when everything worked. Before writing,
  arcedit checks free space, write protection and memory. Ctrl-C stops it
  and leaves the archive as it was.
- No libraries needed.

### Examples

```
arcedit Work:a.lha docs/old.txt DELETE
arcedit Work:a.lha DELETE FROM RAM:list
arcedit Work:a.lha RENAME docs/a.txt AS docs/b.txt
arcedit Work:a.zip docs/a.txt COMMENT "a note"
arcedit Work:a.lzx docs/a.txt PROTECT +s-d
arcedit Work:a.lha ADD RAM:new.txt RAM:Drawer TO docs
arcedit Work:a.zip INFO
```

Template:

```
ARCHIVE/A,ENTRIES/M,DELETE/S,RENAME/K,AS/K,COMMENT/K,PROTECT/K,ADD/S,TO/K,FROM=LIST/K,INFO/S,QUIET/S
```

### For programs that use arcedit

`arcedit <archive> INFO` prints one line, the archive type and the
operations this archive allows, for example
`ZIP: DELETE RENAME COMMENT PROTECT ADD`. It reads only the first bytes of
the archive, so it is instant for any archive size, and lists nothing when
the archive can't be replaced (write protected volume, delete protected
archive).

When an operation fails, the last line of the output is
`Error: <message>`, the return code is 10 (20 when out of memory) and the
archive is unchanged. Return code 5 with `Warning:` lines means some
entries weren't in the archive; the others were done.

### Installation

Double-click Install, or copy C/arcedit to C:. The documentation is in
arcedit.txt.

### History

- @VERSION@ (@DATE@): first release.

arcedit is public domain, all its code is written for it (the Deflate and
